\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The finite Hermitian Gram criterion}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{The \vocabk{reflection form}{fdip-00XF} of a reflection-compatible
functional is Hermitian. On any specified finite-dimensional
half-subspace, reflection positivity is equivalent to positive
semidefiniteness of its Gram matrix in one, and hence every, basis.}

\proof{
\p{Conjugate linearity in the first argument and linearity in
the second follow from the definitions. Compatibility and order
reversal give
##{\begin{aligned}
 \overline{B_\ell(a,b)}
 &=\ell\bigl(\Theta((\Theta a)b)\bigr)\\
 &=\ell((\Theta b)a)=B_\ell(b,a).
\end{aligned}}
In particular each diagonal value lies in #{k_0}.}

\p{Choose a basis #{a_1,\ldots,a_N} of the specified subspace
and write #{G_{ij}=\ell((\Theta a_i)a_j)}.
For #{a=\sum_jz_ja_j}, expansion gives
##{B_\ell(a,a)=\sum_{i,j}\bar z_iG_{ij}z_j.}
Every coefficient vector occurs, so nonnegativity of this
expression is exactly positive semidefiniteness of the
Hermitian matrix #{G}. A change of basis with matrix #{C}
replaces #{G} by #{C^\dagger GC}, where the dagger is conjugate
transpose for the standard Hermitian pairing on coordinate
vectors. The quadratic form itself is unchanged.}

\p{For an exterior half-algebra on ordered odd generators,
the increasing monomials, including the empty monomial,
give the basis. For an even or gauge-invariant subspace,
use a basis of that subspace instead. Neither a nonsingular
Gram matrix nor a sign condition on a fermion determinant
is needed.}
}

\p{The finite-matrix characterization is the component
\leanref{Matrix.posSemidef_iff_dotProduct_mulVec}.
The construction of #{\Theta}, the half-algebra and #{\ell}
is additional data in this theorem.}
